<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah, Staff Engineer here at NAB's Digital Platform team. =
I've been with NAB for about 8 years, working primarily on our customer-fac=
ing applications and build optimization strategies. Before we dive deep int=
o technical discussions, could you tell me a bit about yourself and your ex=
perience with frontend optimization, particularly around bundle size and Tr=
ee Shaking?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah! Thanks for having me. I'm excited to discuss this topic as =
it's something I'm quite passionate about. I've been working as a Senior Fr=
ontend Engineer for the past 6 years, with the last 3 years heavily focused=
 on performance optimization and build tooling.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">In my current role at a fintech =
company, I've been responsible for <strong>reducing our main bundle size by=
 40%</strong> over the past year through aggressive Tree Shaking optimizati=
on, code splitting, and dependency analysis. I've worked extensively with <=
strong>Webpack, Vite, and Rollup</strong>, and I've seen firsthand how seem=
ingly innocent code patterns can completely sabotage Tree Shaking effective=
ness.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What really drives my interest i=
n this area is the <strong>direct business impact</strong> - in financial a=
pplications like we build, every kilobyte matters. A 100ms improvement in l=
oad time can translate to measurable improvements in user engagement and co=
nversion rates. I've actually implemented monitoring systems that track bun=
dle size regression in our CI/CD pipeline, preventing "death by a thousand =
cuts" scenarios.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's impressive - 40% reduction is substantial! At NAB, we serve millions o=
f customers across our digital platforms, so performance optimization is cr=
itical for us too. I'm particularly interested in your mention of monitorin=
g bundle size regression in CI/CD. Before we get into that, let me start wi=
th a foundational question:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Can you explain Tree Sha=
king from first principles? What makes it work, and more importantly, what =
are the fundamental limitations that cause it to fail?</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely! Tree Shaking is fundamentally a <strong>static analysis optimizatio=
n technique</strong> that relies on the principle of <strong>dead code elim=
ination</strong>. Let me break this down from the ground up.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The Core Principle:</str=
ong>
Tree Shaking works by leveraging the <strong>static nature of ES Module imp=
orts and exports</strong>. Unlike CommonJS where <code class=3D"bg-text-200=
/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap =
rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">require()</code> calls can be dy=
namic and conditional, ES Modules create a <strong>dependency graph that's =
deterministic at build time</strong>. The bundler can analyze this graph an=
d identify which exports are never imported anywhere in the application.</p=
>
