<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">In this case, the bundler can st=
atically determine that <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bord=
er-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-=
px text-[0.9rem]">subtract</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border b=
order-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.=
4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">multiply</code> are never referenced, so th=
ey can be safely eliminated.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The Three-Phase Process:=
</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Dependency Resolution</=
strong>: Build the complete module dependency graph</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Usage Analysis</strong>=
: Mark all reachable exports from entry points</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Dead Code Elimination</=
strong>: Remove unmarked exports and their dependencies</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Fundamental Limitations:=
</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key limitation is that Tree =
Shaking requires <strong>static analyzability</strong>. It fails when:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Dynamic Imports/Exports=
</strong>: Anything that can't be determined at build time</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Side Effects</strong>: =
Code that has observable effects beyond pure computation</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Indirect References</st=
rong>: When the relationship between import and usage is obfuscated</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Real-World Complexity:</=
strong>
What makes this challenging in production applications is that these limita=
tions often appear in subtle ways. For instance, even something as simple a=
s this can break Tree Shaking:</p>
